<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernst Haeckel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ernst Heinrich Philipp August Haeckel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernst Haeckel was a German zoologist, naturalist, physician and artist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, who was professor of zoology at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> from 1862 to 1909. He named thousands of new species, nearly 700 radiolarians on one trip to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, mapped a genealogical tree relating all life forms and coined terms such as ecology, phylum and phylogeny. He promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>'s work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and developed the recapitulation theory, now disproven, which he supported with incorrectly drawn images of human embryos. The illustrations of his Kunstformen der Natur influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a>, and he also promoted scientific racism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-darwinism">Social Darwinism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernst-haeckel.jpg" alt="Ernst Haeckel" width="330" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ernst_Haeckel_5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ernst Haeckel was born on 16 February 1834 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>. In 1852 he completed his studies at the cathedral high school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a>. He then studied medicine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, with Albert von Kölliker, Rudolf Virchow, whose assistant he later briefly was, and Johannes Peter Müller. He took a doctorate in medicine in 1857 and received a licence to practise, but after contact with suffering patients the occupation of physician appeared less worthwhile to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1861 Haeckel earned his habilitation in comparative anatomy, and in 1862 he became professor of zoology at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a>, where he remained for 47 years until 1909. Between 1859 and 1866 he worked on many groups, among them radiolarians, sponges and segmented worms, and on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trip he named nearly 700 new species of radiolarians. He proposed the kingdom Protista in 1866.</p><p>Darwin's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-the-origin-of-species">On the Origin of Species</a> made a powerful impression on him in 1864, and in 1866 he published the Generelle Morphologie, which sold poorly. His Natürliche Schöpfungsgeschichte of 1868 had eleven editions by 1909 and 25 translations. His second wife, Agnes, died in 1915, and he died on 9 August 1919.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Haeckel held that the stages of an embryo's development repeat those of its species' evolution, summed up as 'ontogeny recapitulates phylogeny'. He supported it with embryo drawings which he privately called exact and in public described as schematic. Whether they were deliberately falsified or drawn poorly is debated. He did not support natural selection but believed in Lamarckism, and he championed monism, a pantheistic and impersonal view.</p><p>He also put forward a racist doctrine of evolutionary polygenism and claimed that humanity arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, on a lost continent he called Lemuria. Some historians see his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-darwinism">social Darwinism</a> as a forerunner of Nazi ideology and others deny any link; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> banned his books. His Kunstformen der Natur, with over 100 detailed colour illustrations, influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a> movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ernst Haeckel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Germany social Darwinism took shape in the 1860s and 1870s in the exchange between Darwin and his German advocates August Schleicher, Max Müller and <b>Ernst Haeckel</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-darwinism">Social Darwinism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
